- Do you mean "the two elements that occur most frequently (regardless of frequency)?" — then you should count frequencies (e.g., hash map / Counter) and pick the two largest.
- Or do you mean "the elements that can be majority-like (i.e., appear more than n/3)?" — then the Boyer–Moore generalization for k=3 returns at most two candidates in O(n) time and O(1) space (you must verify them in a second pass).

I’ll give both solutions.

1) If you want the two most frequent elements (general case)
- Use a hash map (Counter) to count frequencies, then pick the top two. Time O(n), extra space O(m) where m = number of distinct elements.

Python (simple):
