from collections import Counter

def top2_most_frequent(nums):
    # returns a list of up to two elements (fewer if nums has <2 distinct)
    return [x for x, _ in Counter(nums).most_common(2)]
```

Java (using hashmap, tracking top two):
```java
public List<Integer> top2MostFrequent(int[] nums) {
    Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
    for (int x : nums) freq.put(x, freq.getOrDefault(x, 0) + 1);

    int top1 = 0, top2 = 0;
    int e1 = 0, e2 = 0;
    for (Map.Entry<Integer,Integer> en : freq.entrySet()) {
        int key = en.getKey(), f = en.getValue();
        if (f > top1) {
            top2 = top1; e2 = e1;
            top1 = f; e1 = key;
        } else if (f > top2) {
            top2 = f; e2 = key;
        }
    }
    List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
    if (freq.size() > 0) res.add(e1);
    if (freq.size() > 1) res.add(e2);
    return res;
}
```

2) If you want elements that occur more than n/3 (Boyer–Moore generalization)
- This is the natural extension of the majority (> n/2) algorithm. It finds up to two candidates in O(n) time and O(1) space, then you must verify their counts.

Python:
```python
def majority_elements_n_over_3(nums):
    if not nums:
        return []

    # 1st pass: find up to two candidates
    c1 = c2 = None
    k1 = k2 = 0
    for x in nums:
        if c1 == x:
            k1 += 1
        elif c2 == x:
            k2 += 1
        elif k1 == 0:
            c1, k1 = x, 1
        elif k2 == 0:
            c2, k2 = x, 1
        else:
            k1 -= 1
            k2 -= 1

    # 2nd pass: verify
    res = []
    n = len(nums)
    if c1 is not None and nums.count(c1) > n // 3:
        res.append(c1)
    if c2 is not None and c2 != c1 and nums.count(c2) > n // 3:
        res.append(c2)
    return res
```

